#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A break record that quotes an anchor must still be able to find it.

MISE description="Every quoted break anchor resolves exactly once in its target"
"""
# This exists because three records on `main` quoted anchors that no longer
# resolved, and **not one of them was red**. `scripts/break-once.sh` refuses to
# run when its search literal is not exactly-once in the target, so a rotted
# anchor makes the recorded run unreproducible — while the record goes on
# reading like evidence. That is the failure this task turns into a gate.
#
# The rule it enforces is `break-once.sh`'s own precondition, nothing more:
#
#     text.count(from) == 1        # ANY other count aborts, before any backup
#
# So the check is `count == 1`, and every failure mode is safe to hit by hand: the
# count runs before the backup is taken, so there is nothing written to restore.
#
# ── THAT PARAGRAPH USED TO DESCRIBE TWO STEPS, AND IT HAD THEM BACKWARDS ───────
#
# It read `grep -qF -- "$from" "$file"  # count 0 aborts before any backup` over
# `assert text.count(old) == 1  # count 2 aborts, backup restored`, and leaned on
# the split. **For a multi-line literal the grep was the weaker check, not the
# earlier one.** POSIX gives `grep -F` a pattern LIST separated by newlines, so a
# multi-line literal matches when ANY ONE of its lines does, and a literal ending
# in a newline hands grep an EMPTY pattern that matches every file unconditionally.
#
# Measured against the two-step form, with the literal passed as argv exactly as
# the script passes it: a file holding `alpha` and not `beta` passed the grep, took
# the backup, and failed at the assert with `found 0` and **exit 1** — where this
# paragraph promised a count-0 abort at the grep and exit 2. And **C8's and C11's
# literals both END in a newline**, so for those two rows the grep matched a file
# containing none of their text: the precondition was not weaker there, it was
# inert. Not a rare shape either — 21 of `scripts/replay-fate-breaks.sh`'s 23 breaks
# pass a multi-line search literal, counted by letting bash parse its own arguments.
#
# The two steps are now one count over the whole literal, before the backup.
#
# ── four ways a quoted anchor rots, all four found across three branches ────
#
# 1. **Same file.** A record in the file it breaks can make its own anchor a
#    second occurrence: count 2. NOT always, though - a `///` quote carries no
#    indentation while the code does, so an INDENTED search literal stays unique
#    and the record's copy is not a second occurrence. Same-file quoting works
#    where indentation disambiguates, and fails at column 0 or on a fragment.
#    Where it cannot work, describe the search edit - and still quote the
#    REPLACEMENT, which is always safe because `break-once.sh` never searches it.
#
#    **THE CONDITION, because the registry's unindented-quote habit is a
#    CONSEQUENCE of this mode and was previously written down as a convention.**
#    Whenever `lives_in` and `target` are the SAME FILE, the record's own quote is
#    a candidate second occurrence of the anchor. An unindented quote is safe
#    because the code carries indentation and the registered literal keeps it; a
#    quote pasted WITH the indentation makes the count 2 and the row aborts before
#    any backup. So "quote unindented" is not a style preference - it is what keeps
#    a same-file row resolvable, and stating it as a convention is what made it
#    possible to nearly break by tidying.
#
#    Rows this governs are every one whose `lives_in` equals its `target`, and
#    `C5b` is a recent addition to that set: both fields are `keylink/src/call.rs`
#    and the literal carries twelve spaces. Measured there - **1 indented, 3
#    bare** - so re-indenting either record's quote to match the code would abort
#    it. Do not tidy it.
#
#    **AND ONE ADDED QUOTE CAN REDDEN TWO ROWS, because rows share literals.**
#    Measured over the registry: `keylink/src/idempotency.rs` is the target of
#    THREE pairs that each share one literal - `R15`/`R16`, `A2`/`A2b` and
#    `A3`/`A4`. A count is a property of the LITERAL, not of the row, so a single
#    new quote of any of those three takes that literal from 1 to 2 and **both**
#    rows abort together. This multiplies mode 1 rather than adding to it: the
#    same-file trap says a quote can collide, and this says the blast radius of one
#    collision is two rows in that file, four elsewhere by the same arithmetic
#    (`C5`/`C5b` in `call.rs`, `C8`/`C8b` in `middleware.rs`, `P1`/`P2` in
#    `actor/remote.rs`, `H2`/`H3` and `H6`/`H7` in `.githooks/pre-push`).
#
#    The practical form: **after adding any quote, count the literal and not the
#    row** - then check whether another row shares it **or CONTAINS it**.
#
#    **"Shares" is equality and does not reach CONTAINMENT, which is a ninth coupling
#    the eight pairs above do not describe.** Measured over the registry: exactly two
#    containments exist, and both are inside `H11`, whose five-line literal contains
#    `H5`'s whole literal and `H6`/`H7`'s whole literal as substrings. **So one site of
#    five lines in `.githooks/pre-push` carries FOUR rows.** Built in memory, four
#    perturbations of that block:
#
#      a second copy of the whole block      H5 2  H6 2  H7 2  H11 2   four rows abort
#      a second copy of its FIRST line       H5 1  H6 2  H7 2  H11 1   H6 and H7 abort
#      a copy of H5's line INSIDE the block  H5 2  H6 1  H7 1  H11 0   H5 aborts, H11 GONE
#      a comment added in its `else` arm     H5 1  H6 1  H7 1  H11 0   H11 alone is gone
#
#    Row three is the one to hold in mind: one added line yields `IS NOT UNIQUE (2x)` for
#    `H5` and `IS GONE` for `H11` at once, with different `likely cause` text, and
#    **neither failure names the other row.**
#
#    **The insertion's LOCATION decides it, and that is measured rather than assumed.**
#    The same duplicate line appended at the END of the file gives `H5` 2 and leaves
#    `H11` at **1**, because H11's five lines are still contiguous. Only a copy placed
#    INSIDE the block breaks H11's contiguity. So "duplicate H5's line" is not one
#    perturbation - it is two, with different blast radii.
#
#    **AND THE OPPOSITE CASE EXISTS, so read which one you are in before acting.**
#    `H6` and `H7` also carry load-bearing indentation and for the reverse reason:
#    their `lives_in` is `scripts/hook-guard.sh` while their `target` is
#    `.githooks/pre-push`, so no record quote can add an occurrence to the target
#    at all. Their second copy is IN THE TARGET - an unindented sibling of the same
#    line - so the four spaces are what distinguish two real code sites rather than
#    what separates code from a comment. Same remedy, opposite mechanism: one
#    guards against the RECORD colliding, the other against a SIBLING LINE
#    colliding. A reader who applies the same-file reasoning to a cross-file row
#    will look for a comment that cannot be the cause.
# 2. **The formatter rewrapped it.** A record quoted a one-line expression;
#    `rustfmt` split it across three when the function's signature widened.
#    Count 0, cross-file, nothing anybody wrote by hand. The formatter owns the
#    line breaks and the record does not.
# 3. **A positional anchor.** A record anchored on a slice of `lib.rs`'s
#    `pub use` list. A later wave inserted one more export into the middle of
#    that slice: count 0. Cross-file and nothing rewrapped, so neither rule
#    above reaches it. What moved was a sibling's presence in an ordered list.
# 4. **A fragment, or a line whose text prefixes another.** The standard advice
#    is "pass the line WITH its indentation", and for these it does not work:
#    a `const` at column 0 has no indentation to add; a mid-line fragment like
#    `pop_front()` is not disambiguated by indentation at all; and a line whose
#    indented text is a PREFIX of another line still counts 2 unless the anchor
#    carries the trailing NEWLINE, which no record thinks to mention. So an
#    anchor must be a **whole line, including indentation, plus the newline when
#    its text prefixes another line.** The abort reads as the harness failing
#    when it is the record that is wrong.
#
# The question that reaches all four: **is the anchor's uniqueness a property of
# the LINE, or of its NEIGHBOURS?** — with the sharpening mode 4 forces, that a
# fragment is not a line at all.
#
# ── A METHOD, NOT A MODE: A NUMBER-SHAPED SWEEP CANNOT DECIDE A BORROWED VALUE ─
#
# This one is about records generally rather than about anchors, and it is a METHOD
# because it says how to reach a verdict rather than naming a way something rots.
#
# **A sweep that matches "a number inside a recorded output" cannot tell a BORROWED
# value from a COINCIDENCE. Only tracing the break's data flow can.** A sweep of that
# shape reported five sites as borrowed on 2026-08-08 and **two were OWNED by their own
# test** - one constant and one test argument happening to be the same number, so one
# figure had two candidate owners and no pattern over the text could say which.
#
# **THE COLLISION IS NAMED HERE SO IT CAN BE CHECKED, and citing the sites rather than
# the count is the point.** `MAX_ATTACH` is `Duration::from_secs(30)` at
# `keylink/src/idempotency.rs:266`, and `Duration::from_secs(30)` is ALSO what two tests
# pass as their own argument: `an_attacher_over_the_cap_is_refused_before_it_parks` and
# `a_parked_attacher_keeps_its_place_for_the_whole_wait`, both in `keylink/src/rpc.rs`.
# A `30` in either record's output has two candidate owners. The same shape recurs where
# two limits are equal by intent - `MAX_RUNNING_PER_PEER` and `MAX_WAITING_PER_PEER` - and
# again where a helper in another file owns a `30` of its own,
# `keylink/tests/relay.rs`'s `PATIENCE`.
#
# **THE FIVE SITES, NAMED, so the verdicts can be argued with.** All in
# `keylink/src/rpc.rs`. An earlier version of this note gave the count and named no site
# at all, which made both halves unauditable:
#
#   BORROWED     `a_peer_over_the_attach_ceiling_is_held_for_the_default` - the break
#                replaces the Default, and the test asserts `MAX_ATTACH` by SYMBOL, so
#                the right-hand side IS the constant.
#   BORROWED     `a_peer_under_the_attach_ceiling_keeps_its_own_budget` - the break is
#                `.map(|_| cap)`, so the left-hand side is the default ceiling returned
#                whole.
#   CONDITIONAL  `an_attach_ceiling_below_the_peers_request_governs` - the break makes the
#                cap `MAX_ATTACH`, then the `min` runs against the test's OWN
#                thirty-second request. Both operands are 30, so the figure is
#                reproducible from this file alone and rots only if the constant drops
#                BELOW the request. **Keep it and state that condition**: replacing a
#                genuinely derivable number is a new false claim in the direction of
#                tidiness, which is why three cells beat two.
#   OWNED        `an_attach_ceiling_of_zero_refuses_the_attacher_at_once` - the break
#                DELETES the clamp, so the hold is the test's own request and `MAX_ATTACH`
#                never enters the result.
#   OWNED        `an_attacher_over_the_cap_is_refused_before_it_parks` - the budget is
#                the test's own `Duration::from_secs(30)`.
#
# **The fifth was identified by ATTACHMENT, after a line-numbered citation for it had
# already rotted.** It first reached this file as two line numbers in `rpc.rs`, and
# neither resolved to what it described - one was a closing paren, the other a
# `Ledger::new`. A `///` block attaches to the item that FOLLOWS it, so "which test?"
# has a mechanical answer that no insertion can move: read the block above each candidate
# `fn` and see which holds the sentence. This one's opens *"The cap has to be met before
# the task parks, or it is not a cap"* and contains *"With the bound removed this call
# parks on its 30 second budget"*. The rejected candidate,
# `a_parked_attacher_keeps_its_place_for_the_whole_wait`, opens on a different property -
# *"Taking a place before the park is only half the property: it has to be held for the
# whole wait"*. **Each clause appears in exactly one block, so the mapping is unique
# rather than a best fit**, and that retires the line-numbered dataset entirely.
#
# ── AND THE CROSS-CHECK BELOW IS NARROWER THAN THIS PARAGRAPH ONCE CLAIMED ─────
#
# There IS a check on the verdicts independent of the sweep: **the two BORROWED tests
# carry no literal `Duration::from_secs(30)` at all**, so their 30 can only come from the
# constant. That separates BORROWED from not-borrowed and agrees with every verdict above.
#
# **It cannot discriminate WITHIN the owned set, which is exactly where the ambiguity
# sat.** Measured over `keylink/src/rpc.rs`: **SIX** tests carry their own literal thirty -
# `an_attacher_over_the_cap_is_refused_before_it_parks`,
# `a_parked_attacher_keeps_its_place_for_the_whole_wait`,
# `an_attach_ceiling_below_the_peers_request_governs`,
# `an_attach_ceiling_of_zero_refuses_the_attacher_at_once`,
# `a_forbidden_ceiling_is_refused_without_a_waiting_place` and
# `a_zero_hold_still_spends_a_waiting_place`. So presence of the literal narrows five
# candidates to six, which is no narrowing at all. **Attachment is the check that
# discriminates; presence is the check that classifies.**
#
# **An earlier version of this paragraph presented presence as confirming all the named
# verdicts, which over-claimed its own reach.** That is TWICE now that the paragraph
# arguing a citation must be checkable has carried a check narrower than it claimed -
# first a count with no sites, then a test that cannot separate the case it was offered
# for. Worth saying inside it, because the failure keeps choosing this paragraph.
#
# The deciding step is **which operand survives the break.** A value that is read,
# passed on and then clamped against another can be reported by either side, and
# which side depends on what the substitution does — so it differs per break, and no
# pattern over the text reaches it.
#
# So there are THREE verdicts and not two:
#
#   BORROWED     the record is quoting a value it does not own. Name the constant.
#   OWNED        the test supplies the value itself. Leave it, and say a sweep will
#                re-flag it, so the next sweep does not re-open a closed question.
#   CONDITIONAL  the figure IS derivable from its own file and rots only under a
#                stated change. Keep it and state the condition — **replacing a
#                derivable number is a new false claim in the direction of
#                tidiness**, which is the failure that looks like diligence.
#
# ── AND THE HAZARD THAT TRAVELS WITH IT ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# **A reader who has just been convinced by an argument is the least likely person to
# audit the transcription beneath it.** So check a record against the rule it argues
# for LAST, and never read the argument as evidence that the record already complies.
#
# The general form is worth more than the instance: a record can argue correctly that
# a value must be derived, and then spell that value out a few lines below inside the
# same block. The argument reads as the check having been done. The offending figure
# is deliberately not quoted here, because quoting it would put the borrowed value
# back into a file arguing against borrowing it.
#
# ── THE REMEDY THAT DISSOLVES "THE EDIT SITE SPANS SEVERAL LINES" ─────────────
#
# **`break-once.sh` searches ONE literal and replaces it with anything, so a
# one-line search with a MULTI-LINE replacement is legal — and the replacement is
# never searched, so it cannot affect uniqueness.**
#
# A multi-line edit therefore does NOT need a multi-line anchor. Every row anybody
# has justified with "the edit site spans several lines" was reasoning about the
# SITE when the harness only cares about the SEARCH LITERAL. Three of the five
# conversions on this branch were unlocked by that sentence, and the reason it is
# written here rather than in one row's comment is that its absence is what makes
# the wrong conclusion re-derivable.
#
# So before describing a multi-line edit, ask which single line inside it is
# unique. C14 is the worked example: `.iter()` alone counts 2 and is unusable,
# while the `.filter_map(..)` line one below it counts 1 and anchors the same edit.
#
# ── AND A RECORD IS PART OF THE CORPUS IT DESCRIBES ───────────────────────────
#
# **Writing a record changes the thing the record measures.** The literal you quote
# to document an anchor becomes a second occurrence of it, so the act of explaining
# a break can be what stops the break working.
#
# The asymmetry is worth stating plainly, because it says which edits are risky:
#
#   * REMOVING a quote can only LOWER an occurrence count.
#   * ADDING one can only RAISE it.
#
# **So a requote is the only edit that can turn a resolving anchor into an aborting
# one**, and it does that in the file the author is looking at, while feeling
# careful. Re-reading does not find it; the text looks right. **Count after the
# edit, not after the batch.** And if a count comes back 2, the fix is not "add
# more indentation" — it is that the quote is now byte-identical to something, and
# which occurrence is which has to be seen before choosing.
#
# `break-once.sh` fails safe here: a wrong count aborts at its `grep` or its
# `assert` before any backup is taken, so a bad requote costs a run and not a file.
# But that signal arrives at REPLAY time. The count arrives at edit time.
#
# This is the same insight as the per-tree limitation below, from the other end: a
# record is not an observer of the tree, it is part of it.
#
# ── why this list is written out by hand ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Deriving it would mean parsing prose for "replacing `X` with `Y` in `path`",
# and a heuristic that misses a record checks nothing while reporting success.
# So the list is explicit, exactly as `turns.rs`'s
# `no_failure_prefix_is_a_prefix_of_another` spells out its four prefixes and
# says a fifth has to be added by hand.
#
# **The honest limitation, and it is larger than it sounds: this checks the
# records REGISTERED below, not the records that exist.** A sweep of every
# backticked code-shaped span inside a break record, repo-wide, finds them in the
# hundreds. Most are watched output or replacement literals rather than search
# anchors — which is exactly why no extractor can build this list — but the
# consequence stands: a green here means *the registered anchors resolve*, and it
# is not a statement about the health of every record in the tree.
#
# Registration is therefore **opt-in per record**, and the green line says
# "registered" for that reason. When you write a record that quotes a search
# literal, add it. Nothing will notice if you do not, which is the one hole this
# task cannot close from the inside.
#
# **And it checks ANCHORS, never MESSAGES.** A record's watched output is a claim
# about which assertion fires first, and that rots when an assertion is added
# ABOVE the recorded one — the guard stays correct, the test still fails, and only
# comparing the recorded sentence against a fresh run finds it. No count and no
# resolution check can see that. Two tools answer it, each on its own set:
# `scripts/replay-fate-breaks.sh` on the breaks it hard-codes, and
# `mise run replay-anchors` on the rows `REDNESS` below carries. This task
# deliberately answers the narrower question of whether the break can be STARTED
# at all — and it is the only one of the three that any gate runs.
#
# ── AND THE GUARANTEE IS PER-TREE. IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT A MERGE ───────────────
#
# Every rot mode above is detectable from inside one checkout. **This one is not,
# and no version of this task can be.**
#
# A row can be registered, resolving, replayable and TRUE at branch A's tip while
# branch B rewrites the test the record describes. The literal still resolves at
# exactly one occurrence, so this task stays GREEN on A, on B, and on the merge —
# while the break it certifies no longer reddens anything. Resolution is all the
# checker sees, and the claim died in a file this checkout never had.
#
# Observed, not hypothesised: `C10`'s anchor is a line another branch mutated in an
# experiment and its record is a paragraph that branch rewrote. Its break stopped
# reddening and this task never moved.
#
# **And an `ANCHORS` row cannot even REPRESENT the part that goes wrong.** A row is
# `(id, lives_in, target, literal)` — there is **no watched-message field**. So no
# edit to any row can state the claim that died, and no edit to any row can repair
# it. A reader who accepts this limitation and then goes looking for the row change
# that expresses it will not find one, which is why the absence is written down here
# rather than left to be discovered.
#
# `REDNESS` below is where a row's claim CAN now be written down — a replacement and
# the sentence its break printed, for the rows somebody replayed. **That does not
# lift this limitation.** This task still never runs a test, so a `REDNESS` entry is
# checked only by `mise run replay-anchors`, which no gate runs, and only for the
# rows carrying one. The claim moved from unrepresentable to falsifiable by hand.
#
# So the repair belongs to **the branch that falsifies a row, in the same commit
# that rewrites the file** — otherwise some checkout-able commit has a tree and a
# registry that disagree, and there is no tree from which that is visible. The
# generalisation is worth having: **any registry naming lines in files another
# branch owns is valid at exactly one tree, and the merge is where it silently
# stops being valid.** A green run here is a statement about THIS checkout and
# never about a merge that has not happened.
#
# ── WATCHED BREAKS FOR THE PER-FILE COMMENT MARKER ────────────────────────────
