def flat(text: str) -> str:
    """Whitespace-collapsed, so a literal wrapped across `///` lines still matches.

    A record's quote is broken by the comment's own wrapping; the target's copy is
    not. Comparing them raw finds nothing and reports a false failure - the same
    trap a plain `grep -c` over a record hits.

    ── THE GENERAL RULE, FOR ANYONE BUILDING A SECOND READER OF RECORDS ──────────

    **In this repository every quotable sentence crosses a line break.** The prose wraps
    at roughly 78 to 90 columns, so a sentence long enough to be worth citing is longer
    than a line and is therefore split by definition. **Any checker that matches a
    record's prose LINE BY LINE will report a sentence missing that is plainly there** -
    and it will report it as a zero, which reads as an absence in the tree rather than a
    defect in the instrument.

    This function is why the checks here do not have that bug, and it is stated as a rule
    rather than as this function's own justification because **the thing being built
    around it is a checker that reads records.** Measured on 2026-08-08: two false
    absences in one pass of such a checker, one from a needle spanning a `///` break and
    one from `grep -F` where `.` cannot match an apostrophe. Normalizing the block to one
    line found both immediately. So: **join the block first, then match.** A negative
    result from a line-oriented read of this prose is a statement about the reader.
    """
    return " ".join(text.split())


