# date on a cross-file intersection reads as complete and covers half the input. The
# conclusion survives either way, because it rests on the 0 heredoc lines rather than on
# the carrying counts.
#
# So the over-approximation costs one junk span today and moves no figure. The MECHANISM
# stays: a heredoc holding a shell script whose `#` comment quotes a real code line could
# equal a registered literal, and then it would inflate `blocks_carrying` - overstating
# coverage, the one direction every figure here is written to avoid. `pick` is not that
# shape.
#
# **The commit is named and the currency is NOT claimed, deliberately.** `97b29b7` is an
# immutable handle on the content that was counted, so the figures stay checkable
# forever. Whether that is still the last commit to touch either file is a fact about
# some later tree, and a sentence asserting it would rot on the next edit while reading
# as though somebody had just looked. Re-run the scan rather than trusting the date.
HASH_MARKERS = ("#",)

# Suffixes whose files are hash-commented. A suffix-less file is placed by its own
# shebang instead, because `.githooks/pre-push` is one of the two files this arm
# exists for and has no suffix to key on. Two clauses, one policy - and each clause
# reads the file's own statement of its kind, rather than a list of paths that a
# rename would rot silently.
HASH_SUFFIXES = (".sh", ".py")


def markers_for(path: str, text: str) -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """The comment markers for ONE file, chosen from that file's own kind.

    Rust keeps the three strict markers, and so does anything this cannot place: the
    strict set is the safe default, because it cannot mistake a directive for a
    comment. Widening is opt-in per kind, never global.

    **The shebang is read WHATEVER the suffix, and gating it on a suffix-less name was a
    defect in the silent direction.** It used to require `suffix == ""`, so a `.bash`,
    `.zsh`, `.sample` or `.template` file got the Rust markers even though its first
    line stated its kind - and a shell file read with Rust markers yields an EMPTY
    haystack, which this task reports as a quote nobody wrote rather than as a reader
    that could not read. `.githooks/` is the exposed directory, because git's own
    convention there is `*.sample`. The suffix list stays as the fast path.

    **`#![` IS EXCLUDED, and that exclusion is the whole difficulty of reading a
    shebang.** A Rust INNER ATTRIBUTE - `#![allow(..)]`, `#![no_std]`, any crate-level
    attribute - begins with the same two bytes as a shebang. Dropping the suffix gate
    without this exclusion hands `('#',)` to every `.rs` file that opens with one, and
    then every attribute in it becomes a comment line and its contents become citable
    spans. A shebang names an interpreter, so `#!` followed by `[` is not one.

    Caught by `anchors-selftest`, not by review and not by the tree: no `.rs` file under
    the walk currently opens with `#![`, so **the gate stayed green through the defect**
    and only a case written for the purpose failed. That is the same argument as the
    probes one screen down, arriving on the arm that was supposed to be the safe half.
    """
    suffix = pathlib.PurePath(path).suffix
    shebang = text.startswith("#!") and not text.startswith("#![")
    return HASH_MARKERS if suffix in HASH_SUFFIXES or shebang else MARKERS


def comment_blocks(text: str, markers: tuple[str, ...] = MARKERS) -> list[str]:
    """Contiguous runs of comment lines, marker stripped, each joined into one string.

    `markers` is the caller's, from `markers_for`, and it DEFAULTS to the strict Rust
    set for two reasons. A caller that forgets gets the set that cannot mistake a
    directive for a comment; and `anchors-selftest` lifts this function out of the task
    by name and calls it with one argument, so the default is part of its contract.

    ONE reader, and the unification is the whole point of this function. There were
    TWO: the record-quote check stripped the marker before matching, while the
    population walk kept the whole stripped line. So a quote the comment wrapped
    across two `///` lines flattened to `inner.spent = /// inner.spent...` for the
    second reader and to the clean literal for the first, and the wrapped form
    matched no registered literal at all.

    **LATENT, not firing, so this is PREVENTIVE and corrected no miscount.**
    Measured on the tree this landed on: `blocks_carrying` computes to the same
    figure with the marker stripped and with it kept, because no registered literal
    was quoted only in wrapped form. What the readers did disagree about is the
    hazard surface, measured 2026-08-08 (-03:00) over the blocks naming the
    harness: with the marker kept, 99 of 1630 backticked spans carried an embedded
    `///` or `//!`; with it stripped, 3 did, and all three of those ARE the marker,
    quoted as `///` by a comment discussing one. The denominator read 1629 first, and
    re-measuring it found the reason: **1629 at `96cf374`, 1630 on this branch**,
    whose own edit to `keylink/src/idempotency.rs` adds one span. So it went stale
    inside the branch that wrote it, while 99 held on both trees. That pair bounds
    nothing and is not a constant: it says the disagreement had a surface, and a
    re-measurer on a later tree will get two different numbers.

    Runs stop at a line that is not a comment, which was the readers' SECOND
    difference and was also inert: joining a whole file's comment lines and joining
    each run separately gave identical span sets in every `lives_in` file, because
    no comment block in them holds an odd number of backticks. Per-run is the one
    kept, and not by coin toss. A block is the most text a comment author has in
    front of them, so an unclosed backtick can only pair with another inside the
    same block; whole-file joining lets one in a doc comment pair with one thirty
    functions later and invent a span that no comment contains.
    """
    out: list[str] = []
    run: list[str] = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if stripped.startswith(markers):
            for marker in markers:
                if stripped.startswith(marker):
                    run.append(stripped[len(marker):])
                    break
        else:
            if run:
                out.append(" ".join(run))
            run = []
    if run:
        out.append(" ".join(run))
    return out


def spans(text: str) -> set[str]:
    """Every backticked span in `text`, whitespace-flattened."""
    return {flat(span) for span in re.findall(r"`([^`]*)`", text)}


