# across two `///` lines is still one span - and `comment_blocks` is what joins
# them, the same reader the population walk goes through. This check owning a
# private reader is exactly how the two came to disagree.
def quoted_spans(path: str, text: str | None = None) -> set[str]:
    """Every backticked span inside a comment of `path`, whitespace-flattened.

    The markers come from the FILE, so a record living in a shell script is read with
    `#` while a record in Rust keeps the three strict markers. Before that, a shell
    file returned an empty haystack and every row registered against one reddened.

    `text` overrides the read, and it exists so this function can be EXERCISED. When it
    was added, dropping `markers_for` from the line below left this task printing
    byte-identical output at exit 0 - measured, by making that exact cut - because every
    registered `lives_in` was then a `.rs` file. **That is no longer true: nineteen rows
    live in `scripts/hook-guard.sh`, so the same cut now reddens 19 of them.** The seam
    is kept because it covers the arm for a kind no row has yet - and because a probe
    that drives this function is what proved the plumbing was checked by nothing.
    """
    if text is None:
        text = (ROOT / path).read_text()
    blocks = comment_blocks(text, markers_for(path, text))
    return set().union(*(spans(b) for b in blocks))


